---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import Navigation from '../../components/navbar.astro';

// Page metadata
const pageTitle = "No Babe Art - Serra 2.0";
const pageDescription = "Understanding the foundational principle behind Serra Angel and the Serra 2.0 project";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content={pageDescription}>
  <title>{pageTitle}</title>
</head>

<body>
  <Navigation currentPage="about" />

  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>No Babe Art</h1>
    <p>The Foundational Principle Behind Serra Angel</p>
  </header>

  <main class="container">
    <article class="about-article">
      
      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          Serra Angel is far more than an iconic Magic: The Gathering card. 
          Appearing at the very birth of the game, she embodies one of its visual, mechanical, and symbolic foundations. 
          At a time when Magic was still inventing its own language, this card established the image of the angel within the game's shared imagination: 
          a figure of restrained power, moral authority, and permanence, as envisioned by Richard Garfield, the game's creator.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          For its first depiction, illustrator Doug Schuler was given precise instructions, particularly from Jesper Myrfors, the first art director of Magic: The Gathering:
        </p>
        
        <blockquote class="quote-block">
          <p>
            "When I was art directing Magic, one of my rules was 'No babe art!' That is, no artwork that shows a scantily clad woman in a subservient or weak position. 
            I really did want Magic to appeal to a broader group than traditional fantasy."
          </p>
          <cite>— Jesper Myrfors</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Feminine yet not sexualized, majestic yet humble, armed yet calm; there is no battle scene, no visible enemy. 
          The broad and monumental wings do not express destructive force, but the power of Justice. 
          The sword is not raised, ready to strike, but held as one of her instruments. 
          Doug Schuler perfectly met these expectations, creating an image that would make Serra Angel a defining figure for players around the world, regardless of culture.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          This visual restraint directly echoes the card's mechanics. 
          Serra Angel flies, attacks, yet does not tap. She dominates the battlefield without agitation. 
          The illustration does not seek to depict action, but rather to suggest its inevitability.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          The Angel is often associated with Serra, yet the Angel is not Serra. 
          By choosing to depict one of Serra's creatures, the designers established a myth ultimately carried by the Angel herself. 
          Serra remains invisible, elusive, and abstract, while her Angel fully occupies the imaginative space. 
          This transcendence largely explains why Serra Angel has endured across decades, inspired countless artists, and left a lasting mark on the player community.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          This exhibition, faithful to the original guiding principle: "No Babe Art", revisits this foundational figure through contemporary alterations. 
          Each work enters into dialogue with the original, for Serra Angel is not merely a card from the past; 
          she is an archetype that Magic enthusiasts hope to see endure until the end of time.
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>&copy; 2026 Serra 2.0 Project. All rights reserved.</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>